  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "In Re vs Sh. Bal Mukund on 4 August, 2023 In The Court Of Mr. Sanjay Gargi Principal District & Sessions Judge / New Delhi District Patiala House Courts : New Delhi Fsat06/2022 Cnr No. Dlnd10056472022 In re: Sh. Shatrughan Singh S/o Sh. Rajvir Chand Proprietor of M/s Tomar Paneer Bhandar, 67, Gr. Floor Sanjay Market, Bhag Diwar, Fatehpuri Delhi110 006. ........ Appellant Versus Sh. Bal Mukund Food Safety Officer Department of Food Safety Govt. of Nct of Delhi, 8th Floor, Mayur Bhawan, Behind Super Bazar Connaught Palace, New Delhi110001. ....... Respondent Date of Institution : 04.07.2022 Date of hearing arguments : 26.07.2023 Date of Judgment : 04.08.2023 Appearances: None for the appellant. Sh. Vinod Kumar Sharma, Ld. Chief Pp for the State/Respondent. Judgment:",
    "facts": "It is stated that on 07.10.2017, Sh. Bal Mukund, the Food Safety Officer alongwith other officials visited the premises i.e. M/s. Tomar Paneer Bhandar, 67, Ground Floor, Sanjay Market, Fatehpuri, Delhi110 006 and purchased the sample of \"Paneer\" for analysis under the provisions of FSS Act/Rules/Regulations, for which credit note of Rs. 210/ dated 07.10.2017 was given. The sealed sample packets along with a copy of memo in Form VI were sent to the Food Analyst, Delhi and also to the Designated Officer. An opportunity was given to the appellant/Food Business Operator (FBO) In Re vs Sh. Bal Mukund on 4 August, 2023 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/33921668/ 1 for sending the fourth packet for analysis to NADL accredited Laboratory under Section 47(1)(c)(3) and 2.4.5 of the rule. The Food Analyst analyzed the sample and reported the sample to be substandard because moisture exceeds the prescribed maximum limit of 60% vide its Report dated 12.10.2017. Thereafter, the appellant was provided an opportunity to file an appeal u/s 46(4) of FSS Act within the stipulated period of 30 days, however, no appeal has been preferred by the appellant against the report of the Food Analyst. During the course of hearing before the Ld. Adjudicating Officer, respondent/appellant herein submitted his written statement on 27.03.2018 stating therein that sample was taken on 07.10.2017, on which date, the specification of moisture was according to previous act and amendment for 60% moisture came in the Gazette of India on 13.10.2017 and he was obeying the old directions of 70% moisture . The FSO in his counter reply dated 14.01.2019 submitted that as per the directions dated 02.08.2017, FSSAI has revised standards for Milk and Milk Products and accordingly the Food Analyst has given his report dated 12.10.2017. In all fairness, he has submitted that the Food Safety and Standard Authority of India (FSSAI), which is a regulatory body, has given directions on 02.08.2017 revising the standards for 'milk and milk products'. The notice for operationalization of the amended regulations provided that parameters of milk moisture in Paneer shall be 60% (m/m). Further, these directions were notified by Ministry of Health and Family Welfare on 12.11.2017 and Food Business Operator was required to comply with the provisions of these regulations w.e.f. 01.07.2018 DECISION: 8. As per the impugned order, the sample of Paneer taken from FBO i.e. appellan was reported to be substandard because the moisture exceeds the prescribed maximum limit of 60%. The same is based upon the Food Analyst Report dated 12.10.2017, as per which, moisture in the sample was found to be 63.12%. In Re vs Sh. Bal Mukund on 4 August, 2023 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/33921668/ 2 9. The Ld. Chief PP for the State/respondent has fairly admitted that when the sample from the appellant was lifted, directions issued by FSSAI dated 02.08.2017 were in operation but these directions were notified subsequently on 12.10.2017 and it was further prescribed in the notification that Food Business Operator shall comply with all the provisions of these regulations w.e.f. 01.07.2018. 10. Thereby when the sample was lifted, the moisture contained in the sample was required to be up to 70%.",
